<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.12.4: Writing Equations in Different Forms</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00268</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.12.4: Writing Equations in Different Forms</md:title>
    <md:uuid>87b0cdee-14e3-46ed-9e61-cbe3ffd10b5e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001"><emphasis>Three Different Forms of Linear Equations</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00002">Work with a partner to answer 1 - 3.</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">What is the slope-intercept form of a linear equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\(y = mx + b\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">What is the point-slope form of a linear equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">\(y -y_1= m (x -x_1 )\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">What is the standard form of a linear equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">\(Ax + By = C\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Using the Different Forms to Write Equivalent Equations</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00013">Linear equations can be written in the different forms to create linear equations. Use the linear equation forms from 1 - 3 to help you complete the rest of the activity. Remember to show all of your work on your paper and to use inverse operations to move terms to different sides of the equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00014">For 4 - 5, use the equation \(y-4=3(x+1)\).</para>
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">\(y=3x+7\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00018">Write the equation in standard form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">\(3x-y = -7\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00021">Write \(4x -6y =12\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">\(y=\frac{4}{6}x-2\) orb \(y=\frac{2}{3}x-2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00024">Which of the following is equivalent to \(y=4x +5\) in standard form?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Writing Equivalent Linear Forms</title>
        <para id="para-00025">Write an equation of a line in all three forms that has a slope of 2 and a \(y\)-intercept of 7.</para>
        <para id="para-00026"><emphasis>Slope-Intercept Form</emphasis> Slope-intercept form is \(y=mx + b\) where m is the slope and \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. Substitute \(m=2\) and \(b=7\) to have the equation \(y = 2x + 7\).</para>
        <para id="para-00027"><emphasis>Standard Form</emphasis> Write the same equation in standard form. Standard form is \(Ax + By =C\).</para>
        <para id="para-00028">Remember that \(A\) can not be negative and there should not be any fractions or decimals.</para>
        <para id="para-00029">For this form, rearrange slope-intercept form to lead with the \(x\) term:</para>
        <para id="para-00030"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Bring the \(x\) and \(y\) terms on the left side. \(y-2x = 2x-2x + 7\)</para>
        <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Simplify. \(-2x + y = 7\)</para>
        <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Divide all terms by -1 so that \(A\) is not negative. \(\frac{-2x}{ -1}+ \frac{y}{-1} = {7}{-1}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify. \(2x - y = -7\)</para>
        <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Point-Slope Form</emphasis> Point-Slope form is \(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\). Substitute the slope in for \(m \) and the point in for \((x_1, y_1)\). \(m= 2\), \(x_1=0\), \(y_1=7\) \(y-7=2(x-0)\)</para>
        <para id="para-00035">Notice this can easily be written in slope-intercept form by solving for \(y\):</para>
        <para id="para-00036">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y-7&amp;=&amp;2(x-0)\\y-7&amp;=&amp;2x-0\\y-7+7&amp;=&amp;2x-0+7\\y&amp;=&amp;2x+7\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00037"><emphasis>Standard Form to Slope-Intercept Form</emphasis> Standard form can also be rearranged into slope-intercept form.</para>
        <para id="para-00038">Write \(3x - 5y = 14\) in slope-intercept form. Work to solve for \(y\).</para>
        <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Bring the \(x\) term to the right side with the constant. \(3x -3x - 5y = 14 -3x\)</para>
        <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Simplify. \(- 5y = 14 -3x\)</para>
        <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Divide by the coefficient of \(y\).  \(\frac{-5y}{-5}=\frac{14}{5}-\frac{3}{5}x\)</para>
        <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify and write in the correct order. \( y=-\frac{3}{5}x+\frac{14}{5}\)</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Writing Equivalent Linear Forms</title>
          <!--Q#-->
  <exercise id="exer-00007">
            <problem id="prob-00007">
              <para id="para-00043">Write the equation \(3x +8y =24\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00007">
              <para id="para-00044">Compare your answer: \(y=-\frac{3x}{8}+3\)</para>
              <para id="para-00045">\(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-3x +8y &amp;=&amp;24-3x \\8y &amp;=&amp;24-3x\\\frac{8y}{8} &amp;=&amp;\frac{24}{8}-\frac{3x}{8}\\y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{3x}{8}+3\end{array}\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!--Interaction End --></note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
